<title>POLAND: Foreign stake in Polish privatisation dips in H1.</title>
<headline>Foreign stake in Polish privatisation dips in H1.</headline>
<dateline>WARSAW 1996-10-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>Foreign investors participated in one-third of Poland's privatisations in the first half of 1996, down from 36 percent in 1995 and a high of 66.6 percent in 1992, the Central Statistics Office (GUS) said on Monday.</p>
<p>It said in a report that firms with German capital were most active with 29 acquisitions of either minority or majority stakes, followed by U.S. firms with 13 purchases, Dutch firms with 10, and French, Swedish and British firms with four buys.</p>
<p>The GUS said foreigners were most keen to invest in production firms, especially food processors and beverage producers.</p>
<p>In the first half of the year foreign investors bought majority stakes in 142 Polish companies and further 109 state-owned firms were transformed into treasury-held joint-stock firms.</p>
<p>The GUS did not give a breakdown according to the amounts of capital engaged in the privatisations.</p>
<p>-- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700</p>
